  "query_preview": "..Deep low pressure over the Pac NW will move eastward\nover the next couple of days. Our region will be on the windy and\ndrier south side of the low. A cold front will push into NW\nArizona tonight and move eastward to a Flagstaff-Kayenta line \nFriday morning. The first impact of this system will be increasing\nsouthwest winds with gusts 25-35 mph this afternoon. Winds remain\ngusty overnight into Friday, with the strongest winds along a\nnarrow band just downwind from higher terrain. This includes parts\nof the Kaibab Plateau, the far northeast portions of the Flagstaff\narea (Doney Park, Timberline), and parts of the White Mtns region.\nGusts in these areas could reach 40-50 mph, with most other areas\npeaking in the 30-40 mph range. While the majority of precipitation\nis forecast to stay north of the state, showers are likely tonight\n(60-80%) over the Kaibab Plateau, with lower chances farther south\nas moisture profiles are shallow closer to I-40. Snow levels are\nforecast to fall to the 6000-7000 ft range, with 1-4\" forecast on\nthe Kaibab Plateau. Elsewhere, accumulation (if any) is expected\nto be less than 1\". A trailing shortwave is forecast to move\nacross the area Friday night into Saturday morning. Moisture\nprofiles remain shallow, except across far eastern Arizona where\nthere is a 20-30% chance for snow showers. \n\nDry but continued cool weather is expected Saturday afternoon\nthrough at least Tuesday as the latest model runs persist in\nshifting the next trough farther north for Monday. There is more\nuncertainty in the weather pattern for Wednesday into Thanksgiving,\nwith the next trough possibly moving through. For now, a 15-30% \nchance of rain and high elevation snow showers are included in our\nlatest forecasts. The majority of the guidance shows light \nprecipitation if any, but check back with us for updates for this \nbusy travel period.",
